  "body": "## Summary\n\n- Apply the existing L2 head/batch swizzle to `SingleTileLPTScheduler` in CLC mode.\n- Flatten M clusters, heads, and batches into grid X, with SplitKV partitions on grid Y.\n- Share full-section and residual-section decoding between STATIC and CLC, preserving LPT reversal and guarding dynamic split-metadata reads for invalid work.\n- Add targeted O/LSE tests for section boundaries, short-query GQA, and fully masked causal rows.\n\n## Why\n\nThe CLC path already reverses M-block order for LPT, but it decodes head and batch directly from separate grid dimensions. It therefore bypasses the L2 swizzle used by the STATIC path.\n\nThis change uses a common decoder to group head/batch work into sections based on the existing 50 MiB K/V working-set heuristic. Within each section, head/batch indices vary before advancing through the reversed M blocks. Initial and subsequently fetched CLC work use the same mapping.\n\nM tiles are normalized to cluster units before swizzling, with the CTA rank restored when CTA coordinates are required. An unsuccessful CLC response maps to an invalid sentinel, keeping per-batch split-metadata reads guarded by tile validity.\n\nThe intended benefit is better task ordering and K/V locality. The measurements below establish forward latency differences; they do not directly measure L2 hit rate or hardware work stealing. The public API and CLC enablement policy are unchanged.\n\n## Benchmark protocol\n\n- GPU: NVIDIA B200, SM100; driver 580.173.02.\n- PyTorch: `2.12.1+cu130`; CUDA 13.0; Python 3.12.3.\n- CUTLASS DSL: 4.8.0; quack: 0.6.5; Triton: `3.7.1+gitf797708c`.\n- BF16 dense causal forward, D=Dv, 1CTA, `num_splits=1`, seed 0.\n- Both baseline and patch use `FA_CLC=1`.\n- B: 1/4/16; D: 64/96/128; Hq/Hkv: 16/16, 16/4, 16/1, 5/5.\n- Sq/Sk: 128/8192, 2048/2048, 8192/8192, 16384/16384, 2049/8193.\n- GQA/MQA use packing; MHA does not.\n- Timing uses the repository's `benchmark_attn.py` FA4 setup and `triton.testing.do_bench`: warmup=25 ms, rep=100 ms, mean latency per round.\n- Three rounds per revision; reported latency is the median. Compilation and input creation occur before timing.\n\nThe baseline is `0dc2cb48e484894c48a6cc5c6503fa5cded350c3`. The measured patch is the scheduler change on that revision; the current scheduler source matches the recorded SHA256. The matrix contains **180 shapes and 1,080 measurements**.\n\n## Forward results\n\nSpeedup is baseline median latency / patched median latency. All rows are causal; latency is in \u00b5s. The table includes selected improvements and the largest median regression.\n\n| Shape `(B,Sq,Sk,Hq,Hkv,D)` | Baseline latency | Patched latency | Speedup |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| `(4,2048,2048,5,5,64)` | 49.852 | 35.347 | 1.4104x |\n| `(4,2048,2048,5,5,96)` | 52.980 | 38.070 | 1.3916x |\n| `(1,8192,8192,16,16,64)` | 243.151 | 175.249 | 1.3875x |\n| `(4,2048,2048,5,5,128)` | 54.007 | 39.704 | 1.3602x |\n| `(1,8192,8192,16,16,96)` | 256.118 | 192.422 | 1.3310x |\n| `(4,2048,2048,16,4,64)` | 71.461 | 55.826 | 1.2801x |\n| `(1,16384,16384,5,5,128)` | 352.220 | 357.146 | 0.9862x |\n\nFull-matrix aggregates:\n\n| Metric | Result |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Geometric-mean speedup across 180 shapes | **1.0494x** |\n| Median per-shape speedup | 1.0114x |\n| Shapes with lower / higher median latency | 140 / 40 |\n| Shapes with latency reduced by more than 5% | 48 |\n| Largest median latency regression | 1.40% |\n\n| Sq / Sk | Shapes | Geometric-mean speedup |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 128 / 8192 | 36 | 1.0221x |\n| 2048 / 2048 | 36 | 1.0972x |\n| 8192 / 8192 | 36 | 1.0788x |\n| 16384 / 16384 | 36 | 1.0414x |\n| 2049 / 8193 | 36 | 1.0099x |\n\nThe strongest grouped improvements are B=4 with Sq=Sk=2048 (**1.2470x**) and B=1 with Sq=Sk=8192 (**1.1695x**), each over 12 shapes. Both full and residual sections benefit: the B=4, Hq=Hkv=5, 2K cases fit within a single residual section, while B=1, Hq=Hkv=16, D=64, 8K forms one full section. This is consistent with the intended scheduling change, but does not isolate cache locality from load balancing as the cause.\n\nThe largest nominal speedup, 2.1325x, is unstable: its baseline rounds are 202.419 / 90.652 / 192.948 \u00b5s, versus 90.724 / 90.480 / 90.443 \u00b5s for the patch. It is retained in the aggregate but is not presented as a stable 2x gain. Excluding this one shape as a sensitivity check gives **1.0452x** over the remaining 179 shapes.\n\nClocks were not locked, and the runs span interruptions and a reboot. Incomplete rounds were archived and rerun in full; source hashes and dependencies were checked after restoration. Timing uses ordinary Python calls and CUDA events rather than CUDA Graphs. Three-round ranges overlap for 65 shapes; range separation is not a statistical significance test.\n\n## Correctness\n\nRecorded B200 results for the scheduler patch:\n\n- **27 passed**: existing `TestCLCCausal` cases and `TestCLCFallback::test_local_window_with_clc`.\n- **12 passed**: BF16 section tests covering full, full-plus-residual, and residual-only sections, crossed with causal/local masks and splits=1/3.\n- No failures, errors, or skips in these 39 cases.\n\nThe focused tests use D=Dv=128, Sq=513, Sk=8192. They check O/LSE against the reference, non-finite values, and the executed kernel's scheduler, CLC mode, 1CTA configuration, and split count, including cache hits. Maximum O absolute error was 0.001953125; maximum LSE absolute error was 8.5831e-6, both within the reference-based test bounds.\n\nThe PR adds `tests/cute/test_lpt_clc_swizzle.py` with 32 parameterized cases. The 12 recorded focused passes were obtained before migration into `tests/cute/`; the migrated file has not been rerun on GPU. The remaining FP16 section, short-query GQA, and fully masked-row cases are included as tests, not claimed as executed validation.\n\nRecorded GPU coverage is limited to 1CTA. It does not establish correctness for 2CTA, dynamic split metadata, or the shared STATIC path. The 180 performance shapes were not individually checked against a numerical reference.\n",
